Luxel local patch: frame-atomic framebuffer swap in circular-DMA mode.

Gitea zuckerberg/luxel#376. Upstream esp-hub75 0.14.0 keeps ONE circular
descriptor ring over the whole BCM repetition sequence of the framebuffer
that is currently displayed, and `Hub75::swap` rewrites EVERY descriptor's
`buffer` pointer by the old->new delta, immediately, while the DMA is
mid-pass. Its own SAFETY note admits the consequence: "the worst-case
visual artifact is one partially-mixed frame". The pass is ordered by
plane repetition, so the mix is the high bitplanes of frame N with the low
bitplanes of frame N+1 — visible on the panel as colour corruption at
moving edges. Filmed on the bench panel 2026-09-07 with
library/frame-rate-scan.js: at a sweep-column change, two columns are lit
at once, one of them partially.

This patch makes the swap land exactly on a panel frame boundary.

  * `hub75_dma_descriptors!` allocates `DESCRIPTOR_RINGS` (2 under
    circular-dma, 1 otherwise) rings' worth of descriptors instead of one.
  * `CircularBcmBuf::new` still builds only ring 0, against the initial
    framebuffer. `Hub75::swap` builds ring 1 the first time it is handed
    the second framebuffer (`fill_full_chain`, same layout, its own head
    as the tail's `next`), and thereafter each ring stays bound to its
    framebuffer, so steady-state swapping rebuilds nothing.
  * The swap itself is ONE naturally-aligned 32-bit store: the running
    ring's tail `next` is rewritten to the other ring's head. The DMA
    reads a descriptor's `next` only when it finishes that descriptor,
    and the tail is the last descriptor of a complete BCM pass, so the
    engine either wraps to its own head once more (the store lost the race
    with the tail's prefetch — the flip lands one frame later) or jumps to
    the new ring. Every pass it makes reads exactly one framebuffer. No
    `buffer` pointer is ever touched while the DMA is inside the ring.
  * Only the tail of each ring carries `suc_eof`, so the frame-count ISR
    still fires once per pass and `frame_count()` still counts panel
    rescans.
  * Landing is *observed*, not assumed, and the old framebuffer is handed
    back only once the DMA provably cannot read it again. `out_eof` means
    "the last byte of that descriptor has been read from memory", so the
    question is only which pass the EOF belongs to. Two independent proofs,
    either sufficient:
      - `swap()` reads the DMA channel's current-outlink-descriptor
        register (GDMA `OUT_DSCR` on the S3, registered by the platform
        module through `store_current_desc`). If the engine is still at
        least three descriptors short of the running ring's tail, the tail
        cannot have been fetched yet, so it is certain to read the `next`
        just written and the very NEXT EOF is the switch.
      - Otherwise, two EOFs. By the second the DMA has been all the way
        round the new ring whatever happened.
    The frame-count ISR also lands early if it finds `OUT_DSCR` inside the
    ring that was flipped to. Platforms that register no reader (I2S
    parallel on the classic ESP32) always take the two-EOF rule, which
    costs one extra panel frame of swap latency and nothing else.
    (Measured on the bench panel: with the two-EOF rule the compose rate
    settles at 52 fps against a 115 Hz rescan; with the one-EOF proof it
    tracks the rescan.)
  * When the flip lands the ISR restores the ring it left, tail `next` ->
    its own head, so that ring is self-contained again and ready to be the
    target of the next swap.
  * A second swap cannot be armed over a pending one: correct double
    buffering can't do it (the caller only owns the second buffer after
    `wait()` returns it) and `swap()` debug-asserts, then spins at most one
    panel frame rather than corrupt the rings.
  * A caller that hand-allocates a single ring's worth of descriptors
    keeps the upstream pointer-rewrite path unchanged, and the
    non-circular (`linear`) path is untouched.

Cost: one extra descriptor ring in .bss. On the bench panel
(64x64, 7 bitplanes, 254 descriptors/ring x 12 B) that is 3048 bytes.

Upstream this if it holds (https://github.com/liebman/esp-hub75).

